{
  "id": "80749",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Temperature Dependence of Spontaneity",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When considering the conclusions drawn regarding the temperature dependence of spontaneity, it is important to keep in mind what the terms "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "high"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "low"
              },
              {
                "text": " mean. These are comparative rather than absolute terms: temperatures are deemed high or low relative to some reference temperature, which may vary depending on the situation. A process that is nonspontaneous at one temperature but spontaneous at another will necessarily undergo a change in spontaneity (as reflected by its Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": ") as temperature varies. This can be illustrated by a graphical presentation of the free energy change equation in which Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " is plotted on the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "y"
              },
              {
                "text": "-axis versus "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "T"
              },
              {
                "text": " on the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "x"
              },
              {
                "text": "-axis:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9o0wzde870dm5dn",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "68g2qxupmrgz3q9",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3tw9134ecvraelb",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm\\Delta G\\;=\\;\\mathrm\\Delta H\\;-\\;T\\mathrm\\Delta S",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ekf0e6kbwu1ywos",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "n7arzkfjho8rpp2",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "y\\;=\\;b\\;+\\;mx",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Such a plot is shown in the figure below. A process whose enthalpy and entropy changes are of the same arithmetic sign will exhibit a temperature-dependent spontaneity, as depicted by the two yellow lines in the plot. Each line crosses from one spontaneity domain (positive or negative Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": ") to the other at a temperature that is characteristic of the process in question. This temperature is represented by the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "x"
              },
              {
                "text": "-intercept of the line, that is, the value of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "T"
              },
              {
                "text": " for which Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " is zero:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9n5x0ruk06prrql",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "8airm6mvw4ikwrx",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "rpzlt1b2pgcuh9s",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\Delta G\\;=\\;0\\;=\\;\\Delta H\\;-\\;T\\Delta S\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;T=\\frac{\\mathrm\\Delta H}{\\mathrm\\Delta S}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Saying a process is spontaneous at high or low temperatures means the temperature is above or below, respectively, the temperature at which Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " for the process is zero. As noted earlier, this condition describes a system at equilibrium."
              }
            ],
            "id": "022b2w7f5qqbluv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A graph is shown where the y-axis is labeled, “Free energy,” and \n            the x-axis is labeled, “Increasing temperature ( K ).” The value of zero is written midway up the y-axis with the label, “delta G greater \n            than 0,” written above this line and, “delta G less than 0,” written below it. The bottom half of the graph is labeled on the right as, \n            “Spontaneous,” and the top half is labeled on the right as, “Nonspontaneous.” A green line labeled, “delta H less than 0, delta S greater \n            than 0,” extends from a quarter of the way up the y-axis to the bottom right of the graph. A yellow line labeled, “delta H less than 0, \n            delta S less than 0,” extends from a quarter of the way up the y-axis to the middle right of the graph. A second yellow line labeled, \n            “delta H greater than 0, delta S greater than 0,” extends from three quarters of the way up the y-axis to the middle right of the graph. \n            A red line labeled, “delta H greater than 0, delta S less than 0,” extends from three quarters of the way up the y-axis to the top right \n            of the graph.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "These plots show the variation in Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "G"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " with temperature for the four possible combinations of arithmetic sign for Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "S"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
